In article <C6wH3n.32o@volvo.volvo.se> martin@ant (Martin Borjesson) writes:
>
>What happens if a machine from the net outside the cell wants to reach 
>a machine within your cell? He has only a DNS connection on his side
>so how will he get the IP address for your machine if you're only
>using DCE?

He won't. You still need to run DNS (BIND) so remote machines can find
your IP address for things like mail, telnet, and so forth. CDS does
not address those issues. Similarily, you can't use DNS to find the
exact endpoint of a particular DCE RPC server given its name and
interface UUID -- that's (mostly) what CDS is for.

You can even link different cells using DNS. If I were properly up on
the Internet and you and I were both using DNS as our DCE Global
Directory Agent, you could make RPC calls to my server at
"/.../fig.citib.com/servers/sample_server". In this case the CDS/GDS
code on your machine uses DNS to find my CDS server, then asks it
where the RPC server is.

The DCE Name Service also allegedly supports global naming via
X.400 (or is it X.500? I keep forgetting) style addresses. I'm not
sure how your client will find my server in this case.

