> Ok, ok, cppstdin is a script. Excuse me, imake. Well, can someone explain me
> 1) Why can't I compile AND link with O4 without core dump ?
Documented bug in the release notes as I recall. Use -non_shared with high
levels of optimisation on cc. c89 on the other hand works correctly. (It may
be worth you looking at the release notes as they also point out optimising
bugs - where wrong code is occasionally generated.)

> 2) What is ERROR MESSAGE 4 & 80 ?
Hmmm - odd! Looking in /usr/ccs/lib/cmplrs/cc there is a file err.english.cc
(consisting of bytes offset index and error messages it seems). I'm not sure
if this is actually the correct file to try and guess what messages 4 and 80
mean- anyone at DEC know? Also - do messages start at 0 or 1? A wild guess
from this info would be that error 80 is:
%s:%s: Bad punctuator in the parameter list for #define %s

> 3) Why the program "pncc" can not be found or executed ?
Cannot answer this one. What I can say is that cpp89 uses an environment
variable PNCC_DIRECTORY to look for pncc, however I cannot find a pncc
anywhere. Perhaps you can get away with simply using 'c89 -E' and letting the
compiler do it's cpp business itself in whatever fashion it desires. OR, if
you're still wishing to use a cpp directly, how about using
/usr/ccs/lib/cmplrs/cc/oldc/cpp? It exists and works on my system.

