Since my copy of the DCE RPC is field test, I probably shouldn't name the
vendor concerned, but I've been told they had massive problems getting
the DCE to field test because it uses DES encryption.  Apparently, even
the RPC component without security turned on incorporates DES code, meaning
that its export is restricted...  We don't care about the security features;
as far as we're concerned, the customer should choose whatever level of
security makes them comfortable.

Is anything being done to alleviate this situation?  We do business in Europe,
and I would prefer not to put extra paperwork hassles on our customers because
of an implementation decision on the part of the DCE folks...  Is there perhaps
some alternate encryption scheme developed outside of the U.S?  I find it hard
to believe that DES encryption is the only (or even the best) encryption scheme
publicly available.

Does anyone know the status of export restrictions on code encorporating DES
technology?  Given the non-U.S. (Russian?) implementation of DES already
available, it seems absurd to continue restricting the export of code using
that technology.  The new administration in the white house has presented
the image of being more technologically literate than previous administrations.
Have they done anything on this subject?  Is there lobbying in progress?

Info much appreciated.

Thanks,
- Brad

