Organization: Open Software Foundation
References: <C6Lw8x.5Hy@sugar.neosoft.com>
Date: Thu, 6 May 1993 16:45:17 GMT
Lines: 29

DCE uses Kerberos for its private key encryption.  Kerberos uses DES.  DES
is entangled in various export rules which can, or cannot, be surmounted
depending on who you believe and the current phase of the moon. :-(.

OSF provides an "export" version of DCE that removes the DCE source and
replaces them with a null encryption (i.e., strcpy).  Of course, this null
encryption is (a) useless; and (b) won't interoperate with a secure DCE
host.  However, a vendor could take the export version, give it to a
foreign subsidiary, which then takes "puts back" the DES calls and sells
it over there.  (Apparently DCE export source are in better shape then
Kerberos BONES source, but I haven't looked at that part of the tree
since it was re-orged for DCE 1.0.1.  If you don't know what this means,
don't worry.)

There are no viable alternatives to using DES when you want private-key
technology.  Before you say "what about..." please do a lot of background
work and make sure you have some good credentials to talk about this
subject; it is a very tricky area.  If you think you do have something
solid, and you have had it "pass muster" over in sci.crypt, send me
email.

Public-key solves some problems -- it is exportable -- but introduces
others -- it is subject to US patent.  A European consortium known as
SESAME is planning on doing a prototype that shows DCE using public-key.

The best solution is to get the U.S.Government to remove export restrictions
on software implementations of DES.  You can help; contact the Commerce Dept.
as a start.
	/r$


