Executive summary:
 - DES is required by the current design of DCE; it can be ripped out
of RPC, but the resulting RPC is not useful to the rest of the DCE
core components.
 - it is possible to export DES for authentication purposes in binary
   form with bearable levels of paperwork
 - it is possible to export the DCE source code with the encryption
code removed with no export-control paperwork
 - DES is widely available outside the U.S, and it should be a Small
Matter of Programming for a team outside the U.S. which has purchased
the DCE source code to put DES back into it.
 - it is not intended that anyone ever actually use DCE security
without DES (or an equivalent strong encryption algorithm) in it.

-----------------

In article <C6Lw8x.5Hy@sugar.neosoft.com> daniels@NeoSoft.com (Brad Daniels) writes:

   Since my copy of the DCE RPC is field test, I probably shouldn't name the
   vendor concerned, but I've been told they had massive problems getting
   the DCE to field test because it uses DES encryption.  

   Apparently, even the RPC component without security turned on
   incorporates DES code, meaning that its export is restricted...  

Disclaimer: I am a software engineer, not a lawyer; check with your
company's export control administrators before taking any action based
on this message.

----
That depends on exactly what they built.

There are two compile-time options in the RPC source code:
 - one (AUTH_KRB) removes all support for kerberos-v5-based cryptographic
security if it's not defined.  
 - one (NOENCRYPTION) still uses encryption for *authentication*, but
not for data privacy.

The rest of DCE, including the naming service, depends on AUTH_KRB
being on, and doesn't care about the value of NOENCRYPTION.

If you turn off AUTH_KRB, absolutely nothing in the binary qualifies
as export controlled.

If you turn on AUTH_KRB and NOENCRYPTION, you then have something
which uses encryption only for authentication purposes, which, by the
current U.S. export rules, is "ok", as long as (a) it's exported only
in binary form, and (b) the encryption entry points themselves are
"buried" and not externally accessible.  Point (b) may require a
special tool to "strip" the DES entry points out of the library, but
it's something that can be coded in an afternoon or two by someone who
understands your machine's object file format. (HP happened to have
this support in the linker to begin with so I didn't need to do any
extra work in that area).

You can export the software under various kinds of "general license"
(meaning, you get the license *once* rather than once per customer) to
countries not on the State Dept's "10 most wanted list"..  The
paperwork for the license takes ~2 months, but that can be done *in
parallel* with development/final testing/etc, so if your company has
its act together, it shouldn't delay shipment at all.

   We don't care about the security features; as far as we're
   concerned, the customer should choose whatever level of security
   makes them comfortable.

But they can't choose something which isn't there!

   Is anything being done to alleviate this situation?  We do business
   in Europe, and I would prefer not to put extra paperwork hassles on
   our customers because of an implementation decision on the part of
   the DCE folks...  Is there perhaps some alternate encryption scheme
   developed outside of the U.S?  

There are plenty; many have one or more of the following problems:
 1) they have been cryptanalyzed and "broken"
 2) they haven't yet been subjected to the same degree of analysis as
DES, and may fall victim to (1)
 3) they're patented and require royalties for commercial use.

In any event, you'll have interoperability problems if you use an
encryption algorithm other than DES.

   I find it hard to believe that DES encryption is the only (or even
   the best) encryption scheme publicly available.

Any encryption algorithm strong enough to provide acceptable security
in a wide-area network environment *appears to be* strong enough for
the NSA to consider it a "strategic" algorithm.  "Non-strategic"
algorithms include things like simple substituion ciphers which can be
broken in minutes with paper & pencil, enigma machines (broken by the
allies during WWII), and the RSA "RC2" and "RC4" ciphers with a 40-bit
key size (which is small enough to be vulnerable to exhaustive
search).  In other words, if it's strong enough to be worthwhile, it's
also strong enough to be export-controlled.

The NSA does not consider the availability of an implementation of an
encryption algorithm when they make decisions on whether to allow
export; NSA representatives, when pressed on this, have reportedly
been quoted as saying that they consider their mission successful if
they keep encryption out of the hands of only one bad guy.

Technically, the Dept. of State and/or the Dept. of Commerce make the
final decision, but the reality is that they just do what the NSA
tells them to in this area.

   The new administration in the white house has presented the image
   of being more technologically literate than previous
   administrations.  Have they done anything on this subject?

Yes, they've taken a giant step backwards by releasing the "clipper
chip", in an apparant attempt to prevent industry from standardizing
truly secure encryption technology.

   Is there lobbying in progress?

Yes, there is; the SPA is perenially working on the export control
issue,, and there is an group being organized by the EFF to lobby
against the clipper chip proposal.

There was an interesting, umm, demo, in a congressional hearing room
on 28 April 1993 by John Gage of Sun Microsystems, where he
demonstrated (among other things) a number of absurdities in the
current export control and other laws related to privacy.  I got the
testimony in hard copy form; others probably got it in electronic
form.


