  I should probably enter a clarification here.

> Public-key solves some problems -- it is exportable -- but introduces
> others -- it is subject to US patent.  A European consortium known as
> SESAME is planning on doing a prototype that shows DCE using public-key.

  U.S. export restrictions are a little more complex that this.  The
  Department of Commerce oversees the general exportability list, which
  includes encryption used for AUTHENTICATION (only).  The U.S. Department
  of State oversees the International Trade in Arms Restrictions, which
  include any use of (non-trivial) cryptography for CONFIDENTIALITY and the
  source code or exposed object interfaces that would allow a user to use
  it as such.

  RSA is the dominant public key technology.  Indeed, RSA Data Security
  Inc. controls licensing of the patents in the U.S. (though most major
  computer vendors have already obtained licenses that would allow them to
  use RSA technology in their products).  By their very nature, public key
  technologies are well suited to authentication and it's not very hard
  to ship object-only versions that are restricted to that set of functions
  (and would therefore be exportable once the DoC has been assured that the
  product is limited in this way).  The RSA patents only apply within the
  U.S. (patent laws differ by country and once a product ships, it's no
  longer patentable in many places while the U.S. allows a year to file),
  so RSA-DSI doesn't get into the act when RSA is distributed outside the
  U.S.

  The long and the short of it is that it would be POSSIBLE for an object
  version of DCE to be exported as long as it ONLY used encryption for
  authentication purposes and NOT for CONFIDENTIALITY.  If you need the
  sources, they would have to be stripped of all encryption.

