Rich Salz (rsalz@osf.org) wrote:
: In <C6noC9.AIs@volvo.volvo.se> martin@ant (Martin Borjesson) writes:
: >But, can a DNS get questions answered from the CDS. Can the CDS act like 
: >a full DNS server, from the DNS world?

: No.  CDS only reads information from DNS.

: When CDS is asked to resolve a global pathname like
: 	/.../mycell.osf.org/servers/keyserver
: it asks the GDA to resolve the "mycell.osf.org" part.  GDA, the global
: directory advertiser, says "hmm, looks like a DNS name" so it makes
: a BIND-style query for some TXT fields.  It parses those, and hands back
: to CDS the IP address where it can find a CDS server for the remote cell.

: (Slightly simplified, but you get the idea.)
: 	/r$

So, the conclusion must be that it is difficult to live in a DNS world
since noone on the DNS side can reach information from inside the DCE 
cell? Maybe this can be solved by having having duplicate information in 
a DNS inside a cell??

Isn't this a problem for people living in a interconnected world?

/martin


